------------AI Response------------
Iterator Pattern — Student Example

Imagine we have a Classroom that contains students:

Classroom
 ├── Hamzeh
 ├── Ahmad
 ├── Omar
 └── Ali

We want to go through the students one by one.

Instead of directly accessing the internal list, we use an Iterator.

1. Student
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
2. Classroom
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Iterator;

class Classroom {

    private ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addStudent(Student student) {
        students.add(student);
    }

    public Iterator<Student> getIterator() {
        return students.iterator();
    }
}

The important part is:

public Iterator<Student> getIterator() {
    return students.iterator();
}

The Classroom gives us an Iterator instead of giving us direct access to its internal list.

3. Main
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Classroom classroom = new Classroom();

        classroom.addStudent(new Student("Hamzeh"));
        classroom.addStudent(new Student("Ahmad"));
        classroom.addStudent(new Student("Omar"));

        Iterator<Student> iterator = classroom.getIterator();

        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            Student student = iterator.next();
            System.out.println(student.name);
        }
    }
}

Output:

Hamzeh
Ahmad
Omar
How does it work?

Think of the iterator like a pointer that moves through the students:

Iterator
   ↓
[Hamzeh] [Ahmad] [Omar]
   ↑
 current

After:

iterator.next();

it moves forward:

[Hamzeh] [Ahmad] [Omar]
            ↑
          current

Then:

iterator.next();

moves again:

[Hamzeh] [Ahmad] [Omar]
                     ↑
                   current

hasNext() checks whether there is another student.

So the whole idea is simply:

Classroom
    ↓
 Iterator
    ↓
Student 1 → Student 2 → Student 3

The main point: the code that uses the students doesn't need to know how the Classroom stores them. It just asks the Iterator for the next student.

