The Scenario: The Architecture Problem
Imagine your team is developing the backend for a new music streaming application.
The core Playlist class currently stores Song objects using a standard Java Array. The frontend UI needs to loop through these songs to display the "Up Next" queue, and the audio engine needs to loop through them to play the tracks sequentially.
If you let the frontend and audio engine access the array directly (e.g., using a standard for loop like playlist.songs[i]), your systems become tightly coupled. If you later decide to optimize your application by changing the underlying data structure from a fixed Array to a dynamic LinkedList or a custom database cursor, the entire frontend and audio engine will break because they are expecting an Array.

